#{[17592186128838 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \\to {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v ) \\\\ y ( u , v )\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v) = 2v\\cos{u}$ and $y(u,v) = v\\sin{u}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡u-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\  & =-2v({sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u) \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $$\n\\displaystyle {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]}  = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\\right|_{(0,-1)} = -2(-1) = 2.\n$$"] [17592186128846 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{array} \\right]} \\to {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v , w ) \\\\ y ( u , v , w ) \\\\ z ( u , v , w )\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z) = x^2, \\qquad \nv(x,y,z) = xy, \\qquad\nw(x,y,z) = yz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2x & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =x & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 0 & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & z & y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2x\\begin{vmatrix}x & 0 \\\\ z & y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2x(xy-0) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}y & =2\\cdot x\\cdot (xy) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{u}\\cdot v \\\\  & =2v\\sqrt{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2v\\sqrt{u}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186128847 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{array} \\right]} \\to {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v , w ) \\\\ y ( u , v , w ) \\\\ z ( u , v , w )\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w) =w-u^3, \\qquad \ny(u,v,w) = 2uv, \\qquad\nz(u,v,w) = u^2+v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-3{u}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3{u}^{2} & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2v & 2u & 0 \\\\ 2u & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3{u}^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}2v & 2u \\\\ 2u & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3{u}^{2}(0-0)+1(2v-4{u}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(v-2{u}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} =0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2(v-2{u}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ v-2{u}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v-2u^2 = 0$ defines the parabolic cylinder $v=2u^2.$"] [17592186128848 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{array} \\right]} \\to {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v , w ) \\\\ y ( u , v , w ) \\\\ z ( u , v , w )\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-3-2)+(-1-1) \\\\  & =-10-2 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186128860 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}\\big(\\mathbf{T}(\\Delta)\\big) \\approx \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\cdot \\underbrace{\\textrm{Area}(\\Delta)}_{\\large \\delta{A}}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{v}^{3} & 3u{v}^{2} \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={v}^{3}\\cdot 2v-3u{v}^{2}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{v}^{4}-6{u}^{2}{v}^{2} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-1,2)} & =(2{v}^{4}-6{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\,{|}_{u=-1\\,v=2} \\\\  & =2(2)^{4}-6(-1)^{2}(2)^{2} \\\\  & =32-24 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|8|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =8\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186128861 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}  <    0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u {e}^{v} \\\\ {u}^{2} - {v}^{2}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{v} & u{e}^{v} \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{v}\\cdot (-2v)-u{e}^{v}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-2{e}^{v}({u}^{2}+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{v}({u}^{2}+v) & <0 \\\\ {u}^{2}+v & >0 \\\\ v & >-{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-u^2\n$$\n\ndefines a downward-opening parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/2b/2b6128c5a1737e3ad1fd82f91f5ccd320421ad3472ae8152440034ecea10d364.png)"] [17592186128948 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3},$ then $A=PBP^{-1},$ where\n$$\nB=\n\t\\left[{\\begin{array}{c|cc}{\\color{red}{\\lambda }_{1}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & {\\color{blue}\\text{Re} ( {\\lambda }_{2} )} & {\\color{blue}\\text{Im} ( {\\lambda }_{2} )} \\\\ 0 & {\\color{blue}- \\text{Im} ( {\\lambda }_{2} )} & {\\color{blue}\\text{Re} ( {\\lambda }_{2} )}\\end{array}}\\right], \\quad \\quad\n\n\tP=\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{red}|} & {\\color{blue}|} & {\\color{blue}|} \\\\ {\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}} & {\\color{blue}\\text{Re} ( {\\mathbf{v}}_{2} )} & {\\color{blue}\\text{Im} ( {\\mathbf{v}}_{2} )} \\\\ {\\color{red}|} & {\\color{blue}|} & {\\color{blue}|}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )^{2}-1(-1)) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n$\\lambda_1={\\color{red}-1}.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{(4)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 5}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{-4}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 2\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB \n={\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{red}- 1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\color{blue}- 2} & {\\color{blue}1} \\\\ 0 & {\\color{blue}- 1} & {\\color{blue}- 2}\\end{array} \\right]} .\n$$"] [17592186128976 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n\n﻿﻿﻿$A - \\lambda I = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 - \\lambda  & - 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 - \\lambda \\end{array} \\right]}$\n\nThen, we compute the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-2(-2) & =0 \\\\ 1-2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(5)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 4\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda=1\\pm 2\\textrm{i}.$"] [17592186129006 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \\to {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v ) \\\\ y ( u , v )\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=(u+v)^3$ and $y=(u-v)^2.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3(u+v)^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =3(u+v)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2(u-v) & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2(u-v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3(u+v)^{2} & 3(u+v)^{2} \\\\ 2(u-v) & -2(u-v)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(u+v)^{2}\\cdot (-2(u-v))-3(u+v)^{2}\\cdot 2(u-v) \\\\  & =-12(u+v)^{2}(u-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129015 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \\to {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v ) \\\\ y ( u , v )\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu = 3x-2y, \\qquad v=x+y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}:{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]} \\to {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u ( x , y ) \\\\ v ( x , y )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $3x-2y = u$ and $x+y = v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x-2y)^{2}-y-x=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x + y = v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+x=4\\,⟹\\,v=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &={u}^{2} \\\\ v &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\pm 2 \\\\ v &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2, 4).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq u \\leq 2, \\, u^2 \\leq v \\leq 4\\big\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/0a/0a3d0211dfbe18e5737b62e8b61cf9308af2735ef827d14bae7b5dbfffaa4faa.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = (5)^{-1} = \\dfrac 15.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$  In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}[{\\int }_{{u}^{2}}^{4}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}[v]_{{u}^{2}}^{4}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{[4u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(8-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\left(\\frac{16}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129525 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3{v}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-3{v}^{2})-1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6u{v}^{2}-2 \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,2)} & =(-6u{v}^{2}-2)\\,{|}_{u=1\\,v=2} \\\\  & =-6(1)(2)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-26|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =26\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129527 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v} \\\\ {v}^{2} & 2uv\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 2uv-\\frac{1}{v}\\cdot {v}^{2} \\\\  & =2v-v \\\\  & =v \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,5)} & =v\\,{|}_{u=1\\,v=5} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|5|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =5\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129634 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $$\n\\displaystyle T(\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2u+v) \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186129634 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2u+v)$ and $y=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\frac{2}{2u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{2u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2}{2u+v} & \\frac{1}{2u+v} \\\\ \\,\\text{ }v\\, & \\,\\text{ }u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2u+v}\\cdot u-\\frac{1}{2u+v}\\cdot v \\\\  & =\\frac{2u-v}{2u+v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129660 :question/problem "Which of the following are rotation-scaling matrices?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle C=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186129660 :question/worked-solution "A rotation-scaling matrix has the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & -b \\\\ b & a\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers that are not equal to zero simultaneously. Notice that the entries on the main diagonal are the same, while the antidiagonal entries have opposite signs.\n\nWith that in mind let's check each of the given matrices:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the antidiagonal do not have opposite signs.\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle C=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the main diagonal are not the same.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186129721 :question/problem "![](images/2c/2c835e071e972664ebe02d0046a1bd6652474ce92155709dcb000161ce8f5a2a.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186129721 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is a rotation-scaling matrix. So, we need to find its scale factor and the angle of rotation.\n\nWe can find the scale factor using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{det(A)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent $A$ as a product of a rotation and a scaling matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$  and scale the result by a scale factor of $2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/42/42d0455347222e21192aba7694f1b2eb3f2f9a10cc8a7c8112312003fb2eb9c9.png)"] [17592186129734 :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, then we can write\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the scale factor and $\\theta $ is the angle of rotation. \n\nAccording to the diagram, $r=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186130454 :tutorial/content "![](images/8f/8f2d689080add393fad398d45d992e595996a12c3eca60c1c97e9a3e14720ed1.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant (shown above) bounded by the lines\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{3y-x}} = 3, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{3y-x}} = 6, \\qquad {\\color{red}{2x+y}}=4, \\qquad {\\color{red}{2x+y}}=6.\n$$\n\nThe area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \\to {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v ) \\\\ y ( u , v )\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$  \n\nLooking at the lines that bound our region, we consider the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu = {\\color{blue}{3y-x}}, \\qquad v = {\\color{red}{2x+y}}\n$$\n\nNote that this change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}:{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]} \\to {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u ( x , y ) \\\\ v ( x , y )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=3y-x$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 3y-x=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3 \\\\  & 3y-x=6 & ⟹\\, & u=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v = 2x+y$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2x+y=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4 \\\\  & 2x+y=6 & ⟹\\, & v=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 3 \\leq u \\leq 6, \\quad 4 \\leq v \\leq 6\\big\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/0d/0d55142f7713f3a9bfb93d1b1a383683af397a6edcd7c56a4338f25ab16b83a2.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nOur transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\nu = 3y-x, \\qquad v = 2x+y.\n$$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac17.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{7}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (6-3)\\cdot (6-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133693 :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}+R\\,\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the curl of $\\mathbf F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\n\\mathrm {curl}\\, \\mathbf F =\\nabla\\times\\mathbf{F}=\\left| {\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  x}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  y}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  z}} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{array}} \\right|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ y & {x}^{2} & -2z\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2z)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2})]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2z)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-0)j+(2x-1)k \\\\  & =(2x-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm {curl}\\, \\mathbf{F}$ at the point $\\left(-1,0,2\\right),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\,F)(-1,0,2) & =(2(-1)-1)\\,k=-3\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134114 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">${x}_{1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$8$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$16$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">${x}_{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$6$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$8$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$10$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">${x}_{3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$-10$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$-8$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$-5$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$-1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the hyperplane $y={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multi linear regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?"] [17592186134114 :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$8$** | **$16$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$6$** | **$8$** | **$10$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$-10$** | **$-8$** | **$-5$** | **$-1$** | **$2$** |\n| $y$ | $5$ | $10$ | $16$ | $22$ | $30$ |\n\n \n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -10 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 16 & 10 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 22 \\\\ 30\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."] [17592186134188 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first operation is ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operation is ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{3}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third operation is ${R}_{1}:=3{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$\n\nFinally, to apply the third elementary row operation to ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{3}$ on the left and get ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"] [17592186134208 :question/problem "Let $$\n\\displaystyle u=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},v=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle w=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ be vectors such that $v$ and $w$ are orthogonal. Find $(u+9v)\\cdot w+v\\cdot (u+7w).$"] [17592186134226 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The inverse of an orthogonal matrix $Q$ is given by ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the product $(PQ)\\cdot (PQ)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(PQ)\\cdot (PQ)^{T} & =PQ\\cdot {Q}^{T}{P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(Q{Q}^{T}){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(I){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P{P}^{T} \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the following sum of two orthogonal matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe resulting matrix is not orthogonal since, for example,it is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186134330 :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186134330 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{x} & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134336 :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$"] [17592186134336 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-2)\\cdot 1+(-5)\\cdot 5+0\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=-1,$ $c=1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{-1}{1}=-1.\n$$"] [17592186134387 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">${x}_{1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$5$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">${x}_{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$10$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$25$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$32$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$42$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$15$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?"] [17592186134387 :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$25$** | **$32$** | **$42$** | **$15$** |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $7$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 25 \\\\ 3 & 32 \\\\ 4 & 42 \\\\ 5 & 15\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."] [17592186134389 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$5$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?"] [17592186134389 :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."] [17592186134391 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$5$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression for the vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}$ that best fits the data. \n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\beta =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186134395 :question/problem "Find $2det(A)+4det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186134395 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ is a triangular matrix, its determinant is given by the product of the elements of the main diagonal: \n\n$$\ndet(A)=1\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 1=4\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ has two identical rows, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,det(B)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2det(A)+4det(B)=2\\cdot 4+4\\cdot 0=8.$"] [17592186134397 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first operation is ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operation is ${R}_{2}:=-3{R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"] [17592186134407 :question/problem "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0?$\n\n$$\n|A|=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt[5]{2} & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,|B|=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix},\\,|C|=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186134408 :question/problem "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0?$\n\n$$\n|A|=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 0 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,|B|=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix},\\,|C|=\\begin{vmatrix}4 & \\sqrt{3} & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{5} & 1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186134545 :question/problem "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace spanned by $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186134545 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nIn our case, $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(6)^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186135062 :tutorial/content "Consider the following data set:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$100$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$110$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$120$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$130$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nOur goal is to describe how to use matrices to carry out a linear regression. That is, we want to find an equation of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\n![](images/8e/8ec830e3cf6154675487536afc0e09a42226eb83187b90238a0bfdcefeae5b38.png)\n\nLet's write our problem in matrix notation. Substituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+100{\\beta }_{1}=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+110{\\beta }_{1}=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+120{\\beta }_{1}=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+130{\\beta }_{1}=15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 100 \\\\ 1 & 110 \\\\ 1 & 120 \\\\ 1 & 130\\end{array} \\right]}}^{X}\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{array} \\right]}}^{𝜷}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}12 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 15\\end{array} \\right]}}^{\\mathbf{y}}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix $X$ is called the **design matrix,** and $\\mathbf{y}$ is called the **observation vector**. \n\nSince our points do not lie on a straight line, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nThis approach to finding parameters for a linear regression model is often called **ordinary least squares (OLS) regression**.\n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0.1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line of best fit (regression line) is \n\n$$\ny = 1+ 0.1x.\n$$"] [17592186135063 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to construct a simple linear regression model given some bivariate data $(x,y).$ We can generalize this technique to fit data to an arbitrary polynomial. We call this process **polynomial regression**.\n\nThe following table gives the number of units $x$ (in hundreds) produced by a small company and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) earned for producing $x$ units:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Goods $(x)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Profit $(y)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSuppose we want to find the quadratic function\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points.\n\n![](images/93/93fe1784b89ce4c892c84a03a0dc80909f16f46ac463c5cc30b40f869a7802bf.png)\n\nSubstituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+1{\\beta }_{1}+{1}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=16 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+{2}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+{3}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+{4}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system can be written using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2}\\end{array} \\right]}}^{X}\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}}^{𝜷}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}16 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18\\end{array} \\right]}}^{\\mathbf{y}},\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nSince our points do not lie on a quadratic curve, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0, \\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0, \\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the curve that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=\\begin{bmatrix}22.5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the quadratic curve of best fit (regression curve) is \n\n$$\ny = 22.5 - 9x + 2x^2.\n$$"] [17592186135064 :tutorial/content "When carrying out a linear regression in two variables, say $x$ and $y,$ our task is to find a straight line that best fits a set of data points $(x_i,y_i).$ We can easily extend this technique to situations involving three variables. \n\nThe following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by a small company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Good Type 1 $(x_1)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Good Type 2 $(x_2)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$6$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Profit $(y)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince we now have three variables, $x_1, x_2,$ and $y,$ our task is to find a *plane* of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data.\n\n![](images/ce/ced34ebf098fd20194fafd74f4f75bf855707ad1457e706264c9e922a7efa706.png)\n\n  \n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}+2{\\beta }_{2}=6 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+3{\\beta }_{2}=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+4{\\beta }_{2}=18 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+6{\\beta }_{2}=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs usual, $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the coefficient vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nSince our points do not all lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the plane that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the plane of best fit (regression plane) is \n\n$$\ny = 2+ 7x_1-x_2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNote that we can use the same technique to fit data involving more variables. For example, if we have four variables $x_1, x_2, x_3,$ and $y,$ then we can find a four-dimensional **hyperplane** of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 + \\beta_3 x_3\n$$\n\nthat best fits a set of data points $(x_{1i},x_{2i}, x_{3i}, y).$  The coefficients of this hyperplane are found by solving the corresponding least-squares problem. \n\nFinally, applying linear regression to situations involving three or more variables is called **multiple linear regression**."] [17592186135098 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown below:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large A}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{q}}_{2} & {\\mathbf{q}}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large Q}\n\t\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large R}\n$$\n\nSuppose also that we are given the following system of equations that connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A{:}$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{2}+3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-2{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHow do we find $R?$\nFirst, let's rewrite the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of the vectors $\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\mathbf{q}_3 \\mathbin{:}$\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\mathbf{q}}_{1} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} \\\\ {\\mathbf{q}}_{2} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} + 3 {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} \\\\ {\\mathbf{q}}_{3} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} {\\mathbf{a}}_{3} - 2 {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} + {\\mathbf{q}}_{2}\\end{array} \\right. }\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\t{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1} = {\\color{red}2} {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} = {\\color{red}- 9} {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} + {\\color{blue}3} {\\mathbf{q}}_{2} \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{3} = {\\color{red}8} {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} - {\\color{blue}4} {\\mathbf{q}}_{2} + {\\color{purple}4} {\\mathbf{q}}_{3} .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nIn this new system, the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_1$ correspond to the first-row entries of $R,$ the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_2$ correspond to the second-row entries of $R,$ and the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_3$ correspond to the third-row entries of $R.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large A}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{q}}_{2} & {\\mathbf{q}}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large Q}\n\t\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{red}2} & {\\color{red}- 9} & {\\color{red}8} \\\\ 0 & {\\color{blue}3} & {\\color{blue}- 4} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\color{purple}4}\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large R}.\n$$\n\nTo see why this is, we can take the transpose of both sides of $A=QR{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =QR \\\\ {A}^{T} & =(QR)^{T} \\\\ {A}^{T} & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T} \\\\ \\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{1} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{2} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{3} & \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\end{bmatrix}}^{{A}^{T}} & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{{R}^{T}}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{1} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{2} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{3} & \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\end{bmatrix}}^{{Q}^{T}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we were to expand this matrix product, we would obtain our system of equations."] [17592186135103 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3,$ expressed in terms of its component functions as\n\n$$\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = P(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j + R(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k.$$\n\nIf the first partial derivatives of $P, Q,$ and $R$ all exist, then the **curl** of $\\mathbf F,$ denoted $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F,$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ given by\n\n$$\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial R}{\\partial y} - \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial z} \\right)\\,\\mathbf i + \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial z} - \\dfrac{\\partial R}{\\partial x}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j + \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k.$$\n\nThe formula above is pretty tough to remember. Luckily, there is an easier way. If we define the differential operator $\\nabla$ (a.k.a. the \"del\" operator) as\n\n$$\\nabla = \\mathbf i\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial x} + \\mathbf j\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial y} + \\mathbf k\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial z}$$\n\nthen the curl of $\\mathbf F$ is given by the cross product\n\n$$\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla \\times\\mathbf F.$$\n\nExpressing the quantity on the right-hand side as a determinant, we have\n\n$$\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla \\times\\mathbf F = \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.$$\n\nFor example, suppose that the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=xz\\,\\mathbf{i}+ xy\\,\\mathbf{j}+yz\\,\\mathbf{k}.$$\n\nThen we can calculate $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ using the determinant formula as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ xz & xy & yz\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xz)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)]k \\\\ & =(z-0)\\,i-(0-x)\\,j+(y-0)\\,k \\\\ & =z\\,i+x\\,j+y\\,k\\end{aligned}$$"] [17592186135143 :tutorial/content "Let's find the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ where $A = QR,$ and\n$$\nQ = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}- 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\tR = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}2 & - 4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{b} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nPreviously, we showed that the normal equation corresponding to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where $A=QR,$ can be reduced to\n\n$$\nR \\mathbf{x} = Q^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our matrices into this equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}x & =\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{Q}^{T}}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{b} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4(2) & =2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =10 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ is ${\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}5 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}.$"] [17592186135145 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we might need to place a constraint on the regression plane that forces it to pass through the origin. \n\nLet's once again consider our small company. The following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by the company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Good Type 1 $(x_1)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Good Type 2 $(x_2)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$6$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Profit $(y)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nIt's known that the profit equals zero when the company does not produce any goods, so $y=0$ when $x_1=x_2 = 0.$ The company wishes to find a plane that best fits the data *subject to* the constraint that it should pass through the point $(0,0, 0).$\n\nTherefore, we want to find the plane\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nThe rest of the arguments are the same as before. Since not all points lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution."] [17592186147341 :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives   \n\n$$\n0+2y+4z=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y=-2z,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ and $y=-2z$ into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+2z+18 & =0 \\\\ 2(0)+4(-2z)+2z+18 & =0 \\\\ -6z & =-18 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2z=-6$ and $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,-6,3\\rangle$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y+4z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 1,2,4 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y+2z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 2,4,2 \\rangle.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,2,4⟩\\times ⟨2,4,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-12i+6j \\\\  & =⟨-12,6,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} =\\dfrac{1}{6}(\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2) = \\langle -2, 1, 0 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,-6,3⟩+t⟨-2,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, $a_2 +a_3 = -6+3 = -3,$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle  -2,1,0\\rangle.$"] [17592186147342 :question/worked-solution "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y +5= 0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 2,4,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $3y-2z-7= 0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 0,3,-2 \\rangle.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,4,0⟩\\times ⟨0,3,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\,i & \\,j & \\,k\\, \\\\ \\,2 & \\,4 & \\,0\\, \\\\ \\,0 & \\,3 & \\,\\,\\,-2\\,\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-8i+4j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-8,4,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186147343 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $4x-2y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 4,-2,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $-2x+y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -2,1,0 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨4,-2,0⟩\\times ⟨-2,1,0⟩ \\\\ n & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2 =\\mathbf{0}$, the vectors $\\mathbf{n}_1$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ are parallel. However,  the first plane passes through the origin  $O(0,0,0)$ but the second plane doesn't: \n\n$$\n-2(0)+(0)+1=1 \\neq 0\n$$\n\n So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the planes are parallel but not coincident, they cannot intersect each other.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."] [17592186147344 :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x) = 6\\cos x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos(3x), \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)+3{sin}^{2}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =3\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))}^{′} &=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) &=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for $u_2.$ We start by eliminating $u_1'$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\nu_1' = -\\dfrac{u_2'\\sin(3x)}{\\cos(3x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ -3\\left(-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x) = 2\\cos x \\cos(3x).$"] [17592186147772 :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{5x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{2x})^{′}-({e}^{x})^{′}({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(2{e}^{2x})-({e}^{x})({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}({e}^{2x})^{′} &={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} &={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{x}}=-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ (-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{x}){e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ -{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{e}^{5x}}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{3x}.$"] [17592186147775 :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{5x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{2x})^{′}-({e}^{x})^{′}({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(2{e}^{2x})-({e}^{x})({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{2x})}^{′} &={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} &={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}){e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ -{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =-{x}^{3}{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -{x}^{3}{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =-\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{4x}.$"] [17592186148195 :question/worked-solution "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,2,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+z+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,-1,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨1,2,0⟩\\times ⟨2,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2i-j-5k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186148197 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x-y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,-1,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-z+4=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement “The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$” is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,-1,1⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =i+4j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,4,1⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The planes are parallel” is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true."] [17592186148200 :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n-x+y-2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,x=y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $x=y$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x-2y+z-3 & =0 \\\\ -y-2y+0-3 & =0 \\\\ -3y & =3 \\\\ y & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=y=-1$ and $a=⟨-1,-1,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-x+y-2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-1,1,-2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $-x-2y+z-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-1,-2,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨-1,1,-2⟩\\times ⟨-1,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3i+3j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n\n$$\nb=\\frac{1}{3}({n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2})=⟨-1,1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-1,-1,0⟩+t⟨-1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-1-1=-2,$ and $b=⟨-1,1,1⟩.$"] [17592186148652 :tutorial/content "Now, we’ll apply the method of variation of parameters from start to finish to see the full picture.\n\nConsider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' - 4y = 4e^{2x}\n$$\n\nfor which the corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1 = e^{2x}$ and $y_2 = e^{-2x}.$ \n\nLet’s work through this example using the method of variation of parameters.\n\nWe seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = u_1 y_1 + u_2 y_2\n$$\n\nsubject to the standard constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=4{e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})({e}^{-2x})^{′}-({e}^{2x})^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(2{e}^{2x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $W \\neq 0,$ the functions are linearly independent. \n\nSubstituting $y_1, y_2$ and their derivatives into our constraints gives the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{2x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′} &=4{e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} &=4{e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by solving for $u_2'$ from the second equation to get\n\n$$\nu_2' = -\\dfrac{u_1'e^{2x}}{e^{-2x}}=-u_1'e^{4x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ 2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}-2(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}){e}^{-2x} & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ 2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x} & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x} & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nu_2' = -(1)e^{4x} = -e^{4x}.\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $u_1'$ and $u_2'$ with respect to $x$ (taking constants as $0$):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x=x, \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int -{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(x){e}^{2x}+(-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4x}){e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is $y = y_c + y_p,$ where $y_c = C_1 e^{2x} + C_2 e^{-2x}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =({C}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{C}_{2}{e}^{-2x})+(x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =({C}_{1}-\\frac{1}{4}){e}^{2x}+{C}_{2}{e}^{-2x}+x{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, replacing constants $C_1 - \\dfrac{1}{4}$ with $A$ and $C_2$ with $B,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y(x) = A e^{2x} + B e^{-2x} + xe^{2x}}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants."] [17592186151906 :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of these functions is defined as\n$$\nW(y_1,y_2) = \t\n\t\t{\\left| \\begin{array}{cc}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{\\prime } & {y}_{2}^{\\prime }\\end{array} \\right|}\t\n= y_1y_2'- y_2 y_1'.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W \\neq 0$ for at least one $x \\in I,$ then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W = 0$ and it’s known that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{2}+1 & 2x \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)^{′} & (2x)^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{2}+1 & 2x \\\\ 2x & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2-(2x)\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+1)-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\left\\{ y_1(x), y_2(x) \\right\\}$ is linearly independent on $\\mathbb R.$"] [17592186151907 :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_1(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute $y_1(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot 6x-5x\\cdot 3{x}^{2}+9{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 6{x}^{3}-15{x}^{3}+9{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_2(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute $y_2(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5x)-5x(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2})+9{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ (6{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5{x}^{3}-15{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5{x}^{3}+9{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_2(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Both $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{3} & {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2})-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{5}-3{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & ={x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0, \\infty),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0, \\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."] [17592186151971 :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side is not a polynomial, so we need to use another solution technique. Here, we are given the complementary solution $y_c(t) =  \\dfrac{A}{t} + B,$ which means that we have two fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(t) = \\dfrac{1}{t}  , \\qquad y_2(t) = 1.\n$$\n\nBecause we have two fundamental solutions, we can use variation of parameters.\n\nTo use variation of parameters, we must first reduce the differential equation to the form\n\n$$\ny'' + P(t) y' +Q(t) y = f(t).\n$$\n\nTo do this, we divide both sides of the given equation by $t^2,$ and get\n\n$$\ny'' + \\dfrac{2}{t}y'  =  e ^ {t}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\ny_1(t) = \\dfrac{1}{t}  , \\qquad y_2(t) = 1, \\qquad f(t) = e^t.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)(1)^{′}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  $y_1(t)$ and $y_2(t)$ are independent.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, a particular solution of the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(t) & ={y}_{1}(t)u(t)+{y}_{2}(t)v(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =-\\int \\frac{f(t){y}_{2}(t)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}t=-\\int {t}^{2}{e}^{t}=-({t}^{2}-2t+2){e}^{t}, \\\\ v(t) & =\\int \\frac{f(t){y}_{1}(t)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int t{e}^{t}=(t-1){e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a particular solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(t) & ={y}_{1}(t)u(t)+{y}_{2}(t)v(t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{({t}^{2}-2t+2){e}^{t}}{t}+(t-1){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{2}{t}){e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186151984 :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement.\n\n- To determine whether $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y_1$ and its derivatives into the equation. Computing the first and second derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting into the equation, we get\n\n$$\ne^{x}  - 3e^{x} + 2e^{x} = 0. \\color{green}\\quad\\checkmark\n$$\n\nSo $y_1$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of $y_2$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting into the equation, we get\n\n$$\n4e^{2x}  - 3(2e^{2x})  + 2(e^{2x} ) = 0. \\quad\\color{green}\\checkmark\n$$\n\nSo $y_2$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- Both $y_1=e^x$ and $y_2=e^{2x}$ are solutions of the equation. So, the general solution is $y=Ae^{x} + Be^{2x}$ if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent. To check if they are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}{({e}^{2x})}^{′}-{({e}^{x})}^{′}{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian does not equal $0,$ the functions are linearly independent. Therefore, statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct statements are I, II, and III."] [17592186152567 :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{2x+1} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ {({e}^{2x+1})}^{′} & {({e}^{2x})}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{2x+1} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x+1} & 2{e}^{2x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x+1}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x+1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{4x+1}-2{e}^{4x+1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$"] [17592186152569 :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in R,$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation”; “${y}_{2}(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation” only."] [17592186152610 :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as:\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & ={e}^{2x}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′}-{e}^{-2x}{({e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(-2{e}^{-2x})-{e}^{-2x}(2{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{0}-2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186152622 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the differential equation to the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(t){y}^{′}+Q(t)y=f(t).\n$$\n\nSo, we divide both sides of the given equation by ${t}^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}y=t{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe notice that the forcing term $f(t)=t{e}^{t}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions:\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(t)=\\frac{1}{t},\\,{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}({t}^{2})^{′}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)}^{′}{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2t\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right){t}^{2} \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(t) & ={y}_{1}(t)u(t)+{y}_{2}(t)v(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =-\\int \\frac{f(t){y}_{2}(t)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}t=-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {t}^{3}{e}^{t}\\text{d}t=-\\frac{1}{3}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+6t-6){e}^{t}, \\\\ v(t) & =\\int \\frac{f(t){y}_{1}(t)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{t}\\text{d}t=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(t) & ={y}_{1}(t)u(t)+{y}_{2}(t)v(t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3t}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+6t-6){e}^{t}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{3}{e}^{t} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{3}+t-2+\\frac{2}{t}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{3}){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(t-2+\\frac{2}{t}){e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186152630 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the differential equation to the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(t){y}^{′}+Q(t)y=f(t).\n$$\n\nSo, we divide both sides of the given equation by ${t}^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-\\frac{3}{t}{y}^{′}+\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}y=2{t}^{2}{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe notice that the forcing term $f(t)=2{t}^{2}{e}^{t}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions:\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(t)({t}^{3})^{′}-{(t)}^{′}({t}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{t}^{3}-{t}^{3} \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}_{2}(t)$ are independent.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(t) & ={y}_{1}(t)u(t)+{y}_{2}(t)v(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =-\\int \\frac{f(t){y}_{2}(t)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}t=-\\int {t}^{2}{e}^{t}=-({t}^{2}-2t+2){e}^{t}, \\\\ v(t) & =\\int \\frac{f(t){y}_{1}(t)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(t) & ={y}_{1}(t)u(t)+{y}_{2}(t)v(t) \\\\  & =-t({t}^{2}-2t+2){e}^{t}+{t}^{3}{e}^{t} \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}-2t){e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186153329 :tutorial/content "A **fundamental set of solutions** to a homogeneous, linear differential equation of degree $n$ is a set of precisely $n$ linearly independent solutions.\n\nFor example, consider the second-order homogeneous differential equation \n\n$$\ny''(x) - 4y(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nUsing direct substitution, it's straightforward to show that $y_1(x) = e^{2x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^{-2x}$ are the solutions to the equation. \n\nLet's keep track of our solutions by putting them into a set $S,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\left\\{e^{2x}, e^{-2x}\\right\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the Wronskian of our solutions, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2x}){({e}^{-2x})}^{′}-{({e}^{2x})}^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(2{e}^{2x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not zero, the solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent. \n\nBecause the original equation is of degree two, and since the set $S$ contains *precisely* two linearly independent functions, we say that the set $S$ is a fundamental set of solutions to the differential equation.\n\n**Important:** The fundamental set of solutions is not unique. For example, the set $S',$ given by\n\n$$\nS' = \\left\\{4e^{2x}, e^{-2x}\\right\\}\n$$\n\nis also a fundamental set of solutions. \n\nAs a general rule, we tend to pick the simplest set of linearly independent functions when constructing our fundamental set of solutions."] [17592186157040 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Manhattan distance is defined as\n\n$$\nd_1(\\mathbf a,\\mathbf b) =\\|\\mathbf a-\\mathbf b\\|_1\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $\\mathbf v,$\n\n$$\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|_1 = \\sum_{i=1}^N |v_i|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{1}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{1} \\\\  & ={‖\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}‖}_{1} \\\\  & =|6-4|+|-1-(-4)|+|2-0| \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186157041 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Minkowski distance is defined as\n\n$$\nd_p(\\mathbf a,\\mathbf b) =\\|\\mathbf a-\\mathbf b\\|_p\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $\\mathbf v,$\n\n$$\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|_p = \\left(\\sum_{i=1}^N |v_i|^p \\right)^{1/p}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{4}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{4} \\\\  & ={‖\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}‖}_{4} \\\\  & ={(|6-7{|}^{4}+|-3-(-1){|}^{4}+|2-1{|}^{4})}^{1/4} \\\\  & ={18}^{1/4} \\\\  & ≈2.060,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186157042 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\nd_\\infty(\\mathbf a,\\mathbf b) =\\|\\mathbf a-\\mathbf b\\|_\\infty\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $\\mathbf v,$\n\n$$\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|_\\infty = \\operatorname{max}(|v_1|,|v_2|,\\ldots, |v_N|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={‖\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|-2-8|,|6-(-9)|,|-7-(-9)|) \\\\  & =max(10,15,2) \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186157043 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Euclidean distance is defined as\n\n$$\nd_2(\\mathbf a,\\mathbf b) =\\|\\mathbf a-\\mathbf b\\|_2\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $\\mathbf v,$\n\n$$\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|_2 = \\sqrt{\\sum_{i=1}^N |v_i|^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{2}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{2} \\\\  & ={‖\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}‖}_{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{|7-2{|}^{2}+|2-3{|}^{2}+|4-1{|}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{35} \\\\  & ≈5.916,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186157704 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Manhattan distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{1}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{1}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{1}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{1}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{1} \\\\  & ={‖\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}‖}_{1} \\\\  & =|1-(-1)|+|2-(-1)| \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186157707 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={‖\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|8-(-4)|,|4-4|,|-3-(-1)|) \\\\  & =max(12,0,2) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186157708 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Euclidean distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{2}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}{|}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{2}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{2} \\\\  & ={‖\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}‖}_{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{|5-3{|}^{2}+|1-(-2){|}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13} \\\\  & ≈3.606.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186157710 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Minkowski distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{p}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{p}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{p}={({\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}{|}^{p})}^{1/p}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{3}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{3} \\\\  & ={‖\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}‖}_{3} \\\\  & ={(|1-(-2){|}^{3}+|-1-1{|}^{3})}^{1/3} \\\\  & ={35}^{1/3} \\\\  & ≈3.271.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186157711 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={‖\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|1-(-7)|,|-2-8|) \\\\  & =max(8,10) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186157770 :question/problem "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $4{x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{3}$ is the third component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186157770 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $$\n\\displaystyle [x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing ${x}_{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} & =x\\cdot {q}_{3} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n4{x}_{3}=4\\cdot 2=8.\n$$"] [17592186157791 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-y & -x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186157791 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-y & -x\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}-y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-y)=6x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-y)=-1\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}6x & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186158606 :tutorial/content "![](images/74/74e8a3822098768743be16f0af09d4365d424c83ea473eb56160f4c5f15c5531.png)\n\n        \n\nEarlier, we found that the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to \\big( v(u^2+1), \\: u \\big) = \\big( u^2v+v, \\: u \\big) ,\n$$\n\nmaps the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane shown above. \n\nWe deduced the image of $\\Delta$ by mapping each edge of its boundary. For this method to work, we need to check that $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points in the *interior* of $\\Delta.$ \n\nTo check for critical points, we start by computing the Jacobian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFor the partial derivatives of $x(u,v),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}  = \\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial u}(u^2v+v) = 2uv, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial v} =  \\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial v}(u^2v+v) = u^2+1,\n$$\n\nand for the partial derivatives of $y(u,v),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}  = \\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial u}(u) = 1, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}  = \\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial v}(u) = 0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our partial derivatives into our expression for the Jacobian, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2uv & {u}^{2}+1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(1+{u}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- The Jacobian is negative everywhere in the $(u,v)$ plane. So, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n- Since the Jacobian is negative everywhere, the transformation $\\mathbf T$ is orientation-reversing everywhere (note, for example, that $\\Delta$ and $D$ have opposite orientation).\n\nTo apply this method, we require that $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points in the *interior* of $\\Delta.$ This method works even if the *boundary* of $\\Delta$ contains critical points. Let's explore this a little more."] [17592186158607 :tutorial/content "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\n  \n\nLet's again consider our square region $\\Delta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nNow consider the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to \\big( uv, \\: u^2+uv) \\big).\n$$\n\nThe Jacobian matrix determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}v & u \\\\ 2u+v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSetting the Jacobian equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n-2u^2 = 0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $\\mathbf{T}$ lie on the line $u = 0.$ This coincides with the side $S_4$ of our square.\n\n![](images/6e/6ea454e2a95bf5a9bef1d585c1620ff78a98586c2500293cdfd136c7562a5f83.png)\n\n  \n\nNow, since there are no critical points in the interior of $\\Delta,$ we can find its image in the usual way. However, the side $S_4$ may collapse to a single point. So, let's go ahead and finish this example."] [17592186158608 :tutorial/content "Until now, we've specified that our transformation $\\mathbf T$ should not contain critical points in the interior of the preimage $\\Delta.$ So let's briefly discuss what to do when $\\Delta$ contains critical points.\n\nOnce again, let's consider the region \n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nin the $uv$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\n             \n\nWe now consider the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to \\big( uv, \\: u^2+v^2 \\big).\n$$\n\nLet's start by finding the critical points. First, we compute the Jacobian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}v & u \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2{v}^{2}-2{u}^{2} \\\\  & =2(v+u)(v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe critical points correspond to the solutions of \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x,y)}{\\partial (u,v)} = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points are given by\n\n$$\n2(v+u)(v-u) = 0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u=\\pm v.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $\\mathbf T$ that lie *inside* $\\Delta$ are located on the line $u = v.$\n\n![](images/af/af0cb392b9194a37c7f7e29455e066be52b28e11c8486edc8c992a82d3df625a.png)\n\n             \n\nTo find the image of $\\Delta$ in this case, we divide $\\Delta$ into two regions $\\Delta_1$ and $\\Delta_2$ along the critical line (as shown below) and compute their images separately.\n\n![](images/96/965c24ef46882d40d4ff71b30b2ff60f891298a5dc48a267afc946f719e2b245.png)\n\n     \n\nLet's compute the images of these two regions:\n\n- Computing the image of $\\Delta_1$'s boundary, we find that \n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(S_1)$ is a vertical line segment traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(0,1),$\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(S_2)$ is an upward-opening parabola traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(1,2),$ and\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(C_1)$ is a line segment traversed from $(1,2)$ to $(0,0).$\n\n![](images/c7/c79e3dfcc15a6f5f7c77c59e99d67a29103e77a550a7c53a98b6868d786f5925.png)\n\n     \n\n- Similarly, computing the image of $\\Delta_2$'s boundary, we find that \n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(C_2),$ a line segment traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2),$\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(S_3),$ an upward-opening parabola traversed from $(1,2)$ to $(0,1),$ and\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(S_4),$ a vertical line segment traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(0,0).$\n\n![](images/eb/ebeae2b6425c15fb5670fc83dc64f07a2d407fd108d26c51d8f301d979732cdb.png)\n\n     \n\nSuperimposing the two results, we obtain the following image of $\\Delta{:}$\n\n![](images/28/28993d9cff3621f5b1633940c161aa528ef2b143bafca967cb55bdd9ffa4e1e0.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- As $(u,v)$ runs over $\\Delta$ once, its image $D$ is traced out twice.\n\n- The boundary of $D$ that runs from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2)$ is the image of the critical line $u=v,$ which is *not* a bounding curve of our original region $\\Delta.$"] [17592186161797 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,2,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & - 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3y_1^2 + 2y_2^2 - y_3^2.\n$$"] [17592186161801 :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x},$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-4-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda -9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,\\,-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $A$ has positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186161802 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\Delta_i$ for $i=1,2,\\ldots,n$  denote the principal minors of an $n \\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if $\\Delta_i > 0$ for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n,$ we have $\\Delta_i <          0$ when $i$ is odd, and $\\Delta_i > 0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $a_{11}=-6.$ Expanding $\\det(A)$ along the top row, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-6)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-(-5)\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-6)(-2)+(5)(-7)+12 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-6<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{vmatrix}=37>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-11<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x},$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186161804 :question/worked-solution "Consider the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=6x_1^2+8x_1x_2-9x_2^2.$ To  determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ which is\n\n$$\nA =\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & - 9\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}6-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -70 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =7,\\,-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $7y_1^2 -10 y_2^2.$  \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{y}_{1}^{2}-10{y}_{2}^{2} & =70 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{10}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{7} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{10})^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{10}$ and $\\sqrt{7}.$"] [17592186161907 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the sample mean vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{m} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{x}_i,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{x}_n$ are the individual observations (columns of $X$).\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}, \\quad\n\\mathbf{x}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 5\\end{array} \\right]}, \\quad\n\\mathbf{x}_3 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}, \\quad\n\\mathbf{x}_4 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{4}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186161908 :question/worked-solution "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} B B^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB =\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}- 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ - 5 & 0 & 5\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{3-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 50\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186161909 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the mean-deviation form of the observation matrix\n\n$$\nX \n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}| & | &  & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{x}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{x}}_{2} & \\cdots  & {\\mathbf{x}}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nis given by \n\n$$\nB\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}| & | &  & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{b}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{b}}_{2} & \\cdots  & {\\mathbf{b}}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{b}_i = \\mathbf{x}_i - \\mathbf{m}$ for $i = 1,2, \\ldots, n,$ and $\\mathbf{m}$ is sample mean vector.\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}5 \\\\ 15\\end{array} \\right]}, \\quad\n\\mathbf{x}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}9 \\\\ - 7\\end{array} \\right]}, \\quad\n\\mathbf{x}_3 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB =\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}- 1 & 3 & - 2 \\\\ 12 & - 10 & - 2\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$"] [17592186162346 :question/problem "For the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$"] [17592186162346 :question/worked-solution "For an \"almost\" zero $m\\times n$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1},\\,{\\sigma }_{2},\\,…,\\,{\\sigma }_{r}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal, the corresponding pseudoinverse is the \"almost\" zero $n\\times m$ matrix with entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{-1},\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{-1},\\,…,\\,{\\sigma }_{r}^{-1}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{+} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{-1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {5}^{-1} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186162353 :question/problem "$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X.$"] [17592186162353 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the mean-deviation form of the observation matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | &  & | \\\\ {x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & ⋯ & {x}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis given by \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | &  & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & ⋯ & {b}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere ${b}_{i}={x}_{i}-m$ for $i=1,2,…,n,$ and $m$ is sample mean vector.\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186162354 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $B$ in the mean-deviation form above, where the individual observations are given in columns. \nFind the sample covariance matrix of the data."] [17592186162354 :question/worked-solution "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{4-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}30 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 14\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186162355 :question/problem "$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -10 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data."] [17592186162355 :question/worked-solution "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{3-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}74 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
